  <section class="burn-section">
    <h2>Burn permits in Marion County</h2>
    <div class="permit">
      <p class="juris-season" id="tdf-season"></p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>When you need one:</strong> October 15 to May 15, a free burn permit from the Tennessee Division of Forestry for any leaf, brush, or debris pile within 500 feet of woods or grass. That covers nearly everywhere on the mountain.</li>
        <li><strong>How to get one:</strong> <a href="https://tn.firesponse.com/burn-permit/public-restrictions/">online</a>, in the MyTN app, or by phone at <a href="tel:+18773502876">877-350-BURN (2876)</a>, Monday to Friday, 8:00 AM to 4:30 PM Central.</li>
        <li><strong>Good for:</strong> the day it is issued. The fire must be completely out by midnight. Forestry does not issue permits on days when fire danger is high.</li>
        <li><strong>You may burn:</strong> leaves, brush, limbs, and untreated wood grown on your property. Never household trash, paper, cardboard, plastic, tires, shingles, or building debris.</li>
        <li><strong>No permit needed:</strong> a campfire, a grill, or a burn barrel with a cover of half-inch mesh screen, unless a burn ban is in effect. A burn ban stops all outdoor fires.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="small">Sources: <a href="https://www.tn.gov/tnwildlandfire/prevention/safe-debris-burning/permits.html">Tennessee Division of Forestry</a>, <a href="https://www.tn.gov/environment/air/open-burning.html">TDEC open burning rules</a>, <a href="https://www.tn.gov/tnwildlandfire/prevention/safe-debris-burning/burn-bans.html">burn bans and restrictions</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="burn-section">
    <h2>Before you light</h2>
    <ul class="checklist">
      <li>Get your Forestry burn permit, and check this page and the official status the same day.</li>
      <li>Do not burn when it is windy, when the humidity is low, or when gusts are forecast. Put the fire out if the wind picks up.</li>
      <li>Clear a firebreak to bare dirt around the pile: at least 3 feet through leaves or short grass, 5 feet through tall grass or brush.</li>
      <li>Keep piles small and add to them a little at a time. Stay clear of overhanging limbs and power lines.</li>
      <li>Have a charged hose or water and a rake or shovel at the pile.</li>
      <li>Stay with the fire until it is completely out. Mornings and early evenings are usually calmest.</li>
      <li>If a fire gets away from you, <strong>call 911 right away.</strong></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="small">Burning without a permit, leaving a fire unattended near woods, or letting a fire escape onto someone else's property are crimes in Tennessee, and you can be billed for the cost of putting the fire out. Sources: <a href="https://www.tn.gov/tnwildlandfire/prevention/safe-debris-burning/tn-wildfire-fire-laws.html">Tennessee wildfire laws</a>, <a href="https://www.tn.gov/tnwildlandfire/prevention/safe-debris-burning/burn-bans.html">burn bans and restrictions</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="burn-section how">
    <h2>About the rating</h2>
    <p>The FST Dynamics Basic Fire Danger Rating System puts fire danger in one of five categories, from very low to very high. It combines how dry the dead fuel on the ground is (the 10-hour fuel moisture, measured in sticks about as thick as a finger), how hard the wind is gusting, and how dry the air is. We rate both weather stations and go by whichever shows the higher danger.</p>
    <p>We advise against burning when the danger is high or very high, and whenever there is a burn ban for Marion County, a Red Flag Warning, a Fire Weather Watch, a Wind Advisory, gusts of 20 mph or more, or humidity under 25%.</p>
    <p class="small">This page is guidance from the Elder Mountain Fire Department. It is not a permit and does not replace the official status from the Tennessee Division of Forestry. Data: Elder East weather station (<a href="https://mesonet.fstdynamics.com/">FST Dynamics</a>), Prentice Cooper State Forest weather station (<a href="https://fems.fs2c.usda.gov/">USDA Forest Service</a>), <a href="https://www.weather.gov/mrx/">National Weather Service Morristown</a>, <a href="https://apcb.org/">Chattanooga-Hamilton County Air Pollution Control Bureau</a> (air quality), and the Tennessee Department of Agriculture.</p>
  </section>
